<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bourges</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bourges is a city in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, capital of the Cher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> and of the old province of Berry. As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avaricum">Avaricum</a> it was the chief town of the Bituriges, taken by Caesar in 52 BCE. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was an archbishopric, and its Gothic cathedral of Saint-Étienne is a World Heritage Site. In the 1420s it was the refuge of Charles VII, mocked as the 'King of Bourges'.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bourges.jpg" alt="Bourges" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: KoS · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bourges.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A significant number of oppida became Roman cities, among them Vesontio (Besançon), Durocortorum (Reims), Lutetia (Paris) and Avaricum (<b>Bourges</b>).</blockquote><a href="/culture/la-tene-culture">La Tène culture · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Bituriges, whose name means 'kings of the world', occupied the middle Loire and Cher basins around Avaricum (<b>Bourges</b>).</blockquote><a href="/state/bituriges-cubi">Bituriges Cubi</a></li><li><blockquote>In the division of 511 Clovis' second son Chlodomer received lands along the Loire, with Orléans as his capital, together with Tours, <b>Bourges</b> and part of Aquitaine.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-orleans">Kingdom of Orléans</a></li></ul></section>
